"At the beginning of the surah, Allah swears by the winds scattering dust, the clouds carrying heavy burdens, the ships sailing smoothly, and the angels distributing commands, emphasizing that the promised matters (resurrection, reckoning, punishment) will inevitably come to pass. These oaths draw attention to the importance of the order in the universe and reinforce the certainty of the warnings to be described in subsequent verses.\n\nThen, an oath is taken by the sky, which is likened to a firmly and beautifully woven fabric (Dhat al-hubuk). This simile expresses the firm structure of the sky, adorned with stars and orbits. Following this oath, it is stated that people utter differing words and stray from the right path. While some worship idols, others call the Prophet mad or a sorcerer; and because of these contradictory attitudes, they are turned away from.\n\nThe surah answers those who ask about the Day of Retribution: that day is the day they will be tormented in the Fire. As for the righteous (muttaqin), it is related that they sleep little at night, seek forgiveness in the pre-dawn hours, and allocate a rightful share of their wealth for the beggar and the deprived. According to some exegetes, this right is the obligatory zakat, while according to others, it is voluntary charity. In either case, the importance of goodness and mutual assistance is emphasized.\n\nIt is declared that on the earth and within yourselves there are many signs (proofs) demonstrating Allah's power and the truth of the religion. In the heaven, too, are provision and that which is promised. Swearing by heaven and earth, it is stated that the promise is as certain as your own speech. Then, the story of the angels, who were the honored guests of Abraham, is related: they give Abraham the glad tidings of a son and then announce that they will destroy the people of Lot.\n\nFollowing the story of Abraham, a lesson is urged to be drawn from past nations: Moses (Musa) and Pharaoh, the barren wind that destroyed the people of 'Ad, the thunderbolt that struck the people of Thamud, and the flood of the people of Noah. All of these are examples of the disasters that befell the deniers. The belief in tawhid (the oneness of Allah) is reinforced by reminding that Allah built the heavens with power, spread out the earth, and created all things in pairs. At the end of the surah, a warning is issued that the jinn and mankind were created only to worship Allah, that He has no need of provision, and that the wrongdoers will suffer an inevitable punishment.\n\nAllah is the Provider and the Possessor of power. His vastness bears two meanings: the first is vastness of power, meaning He was not exhausted by creating the heavens and can expand them even further. The second is vastness in wealth and the granting of blessings; He relieves distress and answers prayers.\n\nWe spread out the earth and furnished it with blessings. Live there for a time and take your share. We created pairs of everything. Some understood this as male and female, while others interpreted it as opposites such as motion and rest, or night and day. Al-Baydawi, on the other hand, interpreted it as 'two kinds of every species.' This also encompasses the diversity in the external and internal worlds. There is a duality in everything Allah created so that you may reflect.\n\nCreating everything in pairs pertains not only to opposites in the external world, but also to perceptions in the mind. There is a duality in every perception, and without this duality, nothing can be known. However, this expression 'everything' encompasses created beings; Allah is not included. For there is nothing like unto Him, and He is transcendent above having a counterpart.\n\nTherefore, flee to Allah, protect yourselves from His punishment, and be with the righteous. Indeed, I am a clear warner to you. Do not take another deity along with Allah. Previous nations also called the messengers 'a sorcerer' or 'a madman.' This is a consolation to you. They did not enjoin this upon one another, yet because they were united in transgression, their words resembled one another.\n\nNow turn away from them, but continue to remind, for the reminder benefits the believers. I did not create the jinn and mankind except to worship Me. This is the purpose of creation. Some have interpreted it as 'so that they may know Me.' Worship is for the benefit of the servants; Allah has no need of it.\n\nAllah is the Provider and the Possessor of power. Serving Him is so that one may benefit from His strength and attain reward. For the wrongdoers, there is a large share of punishment. Let them not be in haste; the promised day will surely come. Woe, on that day, to those who disbelieve!"